{
  "requirement": "`habeas add-input --requirements <selection>` MUST add or refresh one requirements input containing the matched full names and their concatenated raw declaration sources. It MUST report the matched names in expansion order and reject a selection that matches no requirement or named example.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Implementation: sound.** I found no behavior gaps and no unmarked code.\n\n- `add_input` and `selectors_for_mode` (src/add_input.rs) turn `--requirements <selection>` into a single `InputSelector::Requirements`. Both carry `{impl}` for this requirement.\n- `InputSelector::resolve` (src/inputs.rs) validates the glob. It then walks the name-sorted definitions map, skipping terms, so matches come out in full-name order.\n- `resolve` concatenates each match's `raw_lines` with one blank line between declarations, and collects the matched names. If nothing matches, it returns the error \"requirements glob … matches no requirement or example\".\n- `to_input` records `{requirements, matches, text}`. It carries `{impl}` for this requirement.\n- `update_inputs` finds an existing input by selector, using `from_input` on the `requirements` string. It then reports `added`, `refreshed` or `unchanged`, and prints \"matching a, b\" in match order, which is the expansion order. It also carries `{impl}`.\n- Resolution happens before any evidence write, so a no-match selection fails without creating or changing a record.\n- Other behavior in this code is covered by other definitions the same code marks `{impl}`: `add_input`, `definitions.capture`, `definitions.dependencies`, and the pending-determination rule in `add_input`.\n\n**Tests: partly covered.** `add_input_modes_capture_outline_nodes_and_refresh_idempotently` (tests/commands.rs) is the only `{test}` for this requirement, and it covers only part of the design space.\n\nWhat it covers:\n- `--requirements 'rules.*'` for both an example target and a requirement target.\n- The recorded `matches` equal `[\"rules.case\",\"rules.ready\"]`, in sorted order.\n- stdout contains \"rules.case, rules.ready\".\n- The record is left with a false determination and a \"Review pending\" rationale.\n- `habeas info` shows the requirements selection and the `md/rules.md:5–7` range.\n- `missing.*` fails with \"matches no requirement or example\".\n- Concatenation with one blank line between declarations is checked indirectly. The validation test in src/evidence.rs (`cited_example_with_a_test_and_matching_evidence_validates`) builds that exact text and expects it to validate. The `requirements_inputs_omit_option_lines` test checks a single declaration.\n\nWhat it does not cover:\n- **Refresh and idempotence for requirements inputs.** The \"add or refresh one input\" obligation is never exercised in requirements mode. No test runs `--requirements` twice for one target, so none checks that the second run reports `unchanged`, leaves the file byte-identical, and adds no duplicate. No test changes the match set or a declaration and checks for `refreshed` with replaced `matches` and `text`. The idempotence check in that test uses only `--item`.\n- **Recorded `text` at the CLI level.** The add-input test never asserts it, only `matches`.\n- **No-match rejection leaving evidence unchanged.** The test checks failure and the message, but not that the record is unchanged.\n- **A single full name.** A selection without `*` is not tested.\n\nThese are the central \"add or refresh\" points of this requirement. The shared update logic is only tested through item inputs, so the evidence does not fully support a `true` judgment. No FIXME comment acknowledges the gaps, so the determination is false rather than FIXME.",
  "inputs": [
